comments[40]:
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T08:37:14.716Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Design (implementation on branch fix/secret-guard-reference-literals-and-property-names). credential_key_assignment now captures a value whole when it holds a run-time reference (braced, command, dollar-name or angle placeholder) or opens with a quoted piece concatenated to more text, and judges the literal left once references expand with CREDENTIAL_LITERAL_VALUE: the unchanged reference-free grammar (paths, URLs, words, member paths, key-file names excluded; 8+ characters; entropy 3) read as one double-quoted literal, because the literal is data. The rule gains minimumLiteralLength 8 and a new declarative SecretRule field literalValue; acceptsSecretMatch judges the literal (not the captured text) for entropy and rejectValue whenever a rule declares a literal minimum. judgedLiteral treats a capture whose quotes the grammar read outside it (a quoted credential-key body) as data, so its quote characters are literal. Bounds: at most 64 literal characters before the first reference (a longer prefix is judged as a reference-free value), references and quoted pieces are whitespace-free (so a spaced command substitution is never one value and a key inside it keeps its own verdict), every repeated group is bounded at 4096 and quoted pieces at 4, as in the password grammar. Bare dollar-name policy (one policy for both key-named rules): a dollar-name reference expands to nothing when the name is CONSTANT_CASE (environment-variable convention) or when it is the whole value, quoted or not; bareness is judged on the source text, so a concatenation never reads as one name. Any other dollar text is literal, so a credential that merely contains a dollar sign keeps its characters."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T08:37:34.904Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "False-positive evidence (AC3): scanned every title, description, close field, comment, note and learning of this tracker (47,292 texts, 3,323 items) with scanMutationSecrets before and after the change. Before: credential_key_assignment 8 hits, password_assignment 4. After: identical counts on the identical items, all synthetic samples quoted in the secret-guard items themselves. The wider grammar added zero hits on real agent text. The GH-1704 must-allow corpus in secret-policy-parity.spec.ts and the whole-value-reference allow corpus stay clean."
    edited_at: "2026-11-17T08:38:03.174Z"
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T09:26:19.802Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 1 on PR 1746 (gpt-6.1-sol, xhigh) found two credential-key regressions against main, both fixed. First: a quoted credential followed by member access was read as a concatenation, and the literal then looked like a dotted member path. Concatenation now opens only when the quoted piece holds a reference, or a reference or another quoted piece follows it directly, so reference-free code keeps its quoted-value reading. Second: a key inside a rejected referenced value, after a variable-led path, inside an angle placeholder, or two references deep, was hidden from the flat scan. The rule now declares a baseline reading (new SecretRule.baseline: its own flat pattern and structured value grammar), and the unchanged reference-free grammar is scanned too in text, structured, element and payload-member spellings. A value is reported when either reading reports it, so the referenced reading only adds findings and every key the reference-free scan visits keeps its verdict. Regression rows were added for all five shapes, plus one keeping a quoted prefix concatenated with code clean."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T09:41:29.664Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 2 on PR 1746 found that the first dollar-name policy (every CONSTANT_CASE name expands to nothing) dropped a credential that contains a dollar sign followed by an uppercase run after other characters, including an escaped dollar in a quoted value, which main reports. The policy is narrowed to the smallest change from main: a CONSTANT_CASE dollar-name expands to nothing only when no literal text precedes it in its word (the value or a default word), and a bare dollar-name of any case (the whole value) still spells nothing. Any other dollar text is literal. This supersedes the policy sentence in the close resolution. Rows were added for a CONSTANT_CASE name after literal text, an escaped dollar in a quoted value, and the payload-member spelling. The tracker false-positive scan is still 8 and 4 hits."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T09:58:50.342Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 3 on PR 1746 found two more regressions against main, both fixed. First, the baseline reading was judged with the new reference-expanding predicates, so a quoted value whose command substitution holds an assignment spelled two characters and was dropped. The baseline is now judged exactly as main judged it: on the captured text, with the value and entropy predicates but no literal minimum or literal grammar. Credential-key findings are therefore structurally a superset of main's. Second, a bare or leading dollar-name was decided on a capture that the password grammar had cut short at a colon, slash or question mark, so a password variable followed by a literal suffix was dropped. A dollar-name now counts as a reference only when the value grammar read the whole word, that is, when the match ends the input or a word-ending character follows it; otherwise its text stays literal, as on main. Rows were added for both shapes. Timing fixtures were also halved after an instrumented run on a host at load 15 to 20 came close to the 5-second bound."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T10:13:15.600Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 4 on PR 1746 found a missed literal and a new false positive, both fixed. (1) Word completeness trusted a following bracket, brace or comma as the end of a shell word, so a password variable followed by a bracket and a literal suffix was dropped. A word now counts as read whole only when nothing but punctuation follows the match before whitespace or the end of the text. The look-ahead is bounded at 64 characters, so judging stays linear. (2) A quoted reference followed by a method call opened a concatenation, and the method name became the apparent credential. Concatenation after a quoted piece that holds a reference now needs a letter, digit or underscore next. A literal that is only a member chain left after a reference is treated as code. Rows were added for bracket, brace, comma and parenthesis suffixes, a JSON string holding a bare variable, and three code-after-reference shapes, all clean or flagged as main reports them."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T10:26:34.498Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 5 on PR 1746 found a new false positive, fixed. After the equals sign, the referenced reading read the shell control operators as value characters, so a command substitution followed directly by a double pipe and a fallback command spelled the fallback as a credential. The bar and ampersand now end a referenced value, because they end a shell word. The baseline reading still reads them in a reference-free value, as main does. Rows were added for a double pipe, a double ampersand and a pipe after a variable."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T10:42:50.248Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Review round on PR 1746 (Greptile four P1s on intermediate heads, Codex round 6 two P2s), all fixed. (1) A referenced credential inside a rejected referenced value was no longer judged once rescans were limited to the password rule. credential_key_assignment now rescans a rejected referenced value shallowly: each inner key is judged once with the referenced grammar and the scan resumes after it, so each character is read at most twice. The password rule keeps the deep rescan. (2) A punctuation-led literal after a quoted reference was dropped by the alphanumeric-only continuation; the continuation now also accepts the non-operator symbols _ ! @ # % ^ ~ -. (3) The member-chain exclusion applied to payload data and quoted values too; it now applies only to unquoted source text. (4) In compact JSON the word look-ahead ran into the next member, so a bare variable was flagged. A closed quoted value followed by structure (a comma, colon, bracket, brace or semicolon, then whitespace, a quote, more structure or the end) now ends its word. (5) The 64-character look-ahead matched code points but was bounded in code units, so astral characters could make a truncated slice read as the end of the input. It now finds the punctuation run and checks the input at that exact code-unit position. (6) Question marks and colons after a reference are code (optional chaining, nullish coalescing, ternary), so they now end a referenced value, as the bar and ampersand do. Rows were added for every shape, including both JSON member orders."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T11:11:20.718Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Greptile on e8226f18b raised two more P1s, both fixed; neither is a regression against main. (1) Ending referenced values at every ampersand cut YAML data such as a reference followed by a literal holding a single ampersand. Only the double ampersand, the double bar and the pipe now end a referenced value; a single ampersand is data. Each run alternative is one character, so runs still cannot be split. (2) After a closing quote, two structure characters were accepted without checking what followed, so a quoted variable followed by a bracket, a comma and a literal lost the literal. The structure run (bounded at 64) must now be followed by whitespace, a quote or the end. Rows were added for both shapes and for a JSON array member that stays clean. Local note: stopping a running static gate leaves .cache/build-lease behind; the lease is the directory, so after confirming that no producer or consumer is alive, the empty directory must be removed, not only owner.json."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T11:28:01.869Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 8 on PR 1746 found two more boundary shifts against main, fixed by making the rules simpler, not wider. (1) Word completeness accepted any punctuation after a password variable, so a variable followed by a slash-and-plus suffix lost the suffix main reports. A word now ends only at whitespace or the end of the text, optionally after its closing quote and a bounded run of structure (semicolon, comma, closing parenthesis, brace or bracket). Any other text after the match is part of the same word. (2) The code exclusion required the whole literal to be a member chain, so member access on a reference followed by arithmetic became a finding. In unquoted source, a literal that starts with member access on a reference is now code."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T11:43:43.930Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Greptile on e4970e93a and Codex review round 9 on PR 1746 pulled the word-end rule in opposite directions: nested compact JSON with a bare variable was flagged, and an unquoted variable followed by an opening bracket, a brace or a comma and a quoted piece lost its literal suffix. The rule now separates the two cases. An unquoted value ends its word only at whitespace or the end of the text, optionally after structure. A quoted value, whose closing quote ends the capture or directly follows it, also ends its word when at least one structure character and then a quote, brace or bracket follow, as the next JSON member or element does. Two quoted pieces with nothing between them stay one shell word. Rows were added for every shape from both reviews."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T11:59:06.037Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 10 found a P1 regression against main: a quote after an unquoted password value was taken to close an enclosing string, but it can open a spaced concatenated piece, so a bare-looking variable followed by a quoted passphrase was dropped. A quote now counts as closing only when the grammar read the value as quoted: the capture ends with the closing quote, or its opening quote precedes the capture and the closing quote follows it. Otherwise the variable keeps main's literal reading. This deliberately gives back one earlier allowance: a bare variable directly before the closing quote of a string that holds a whole assignment is judged as on main. Greptile's member-access slip is fixed too: the exclusion now applies only when the capture starts with a reference, so a dotted literal that begins before the reference is judged. Both remaining CodeRabbit minors on e4970e93a are recorded on pm-jcz7qz rather than widened here, because main misses both shapes as well."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T12:19:51.799Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Greptile on fe8d8e598: the opening-brace-or-bracket allowance after structure also ended a shell word that continues with a bracket and literal text, so a quoted variable followed by a closing bracket, an opening bracket and a literal lost the literal that main reports. A brace or bracket now counts as the start of the next JSON element only when structure, a quote, whitespace or the end follows it. Rows were added for both bracket shapes and for a nested array element that stays clean. Codex review round 11 on c529018a9 found no actionable regressions."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T12:36:55.931Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Greptile on 40b325990: the stricter next-element check rejected a nested array that starts with a JSON number or literal, so compact JSON with a bare variable followed by such an array was flagged. After a brace or bracket the check now also accepts a JSON number, true, false or null followed by a non-word character, so a bracket followed by word text that merely starts with true still continues the shell word. Rows cover numbers, negative numbers, true, null, an empty object, both quote kinds, and the true-prefixed literal."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T12:50:58.725Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 13 on PR 1746, two fixes. (1) A JSON number after a bracket was recognized from its first digit alone, so a bracket followed by a digit-led literal ended the shell word. A number, true, false or null now counts only as a complete token ended by a comma, closing bracket, closing brace or whitespace, within a 40-character look-ahead. (2) A TypeScript type assertion before a call left the function name as the literal after an angle placeholder. In unquoted source, a referenced value that a call's opening parenthesis follows is now code, like member access on a reference."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T13:03:22.015Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 14 on PR 1746, four fixes. (1) P1: after an unquoted key, a quoted variable followed by a comma and a quoted piece was taken for a JSON member; that is one shell word. The next-member shortcut now holds only when the value's own key is quoted, as in JSON, YAML flow or a Python dict. (2) A key inside a rejected command substitution, whose value ends at the substitution's closing parenthesis before the enclosing quote, kept its variable as literal text; a closing parenthesis or brace followed by a quote now ends an unquoted value's word. (3) The constant-statement exclusion tested the raw capture, so a type assertion before a constant was flagged; it now tests the derived literal (unchanged for the baseline reading). (4) Call detection now allows up to 16 blanks before the parenthesis."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T13:14:36.888Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 15 on PR 1746, two fixes. (1) The closing-parenthesis-or-brace word-end shortcut also applied at the top level, where such a character before a quoted literal is data, so a password variable followed by a brace and a quoted literal lost the literal. It now applies only to a match the rescan found inside a rejected reference value; those matches are tracked explicitly. (2) A type assertion before an optional call, a nullish fallback or a statement end left the identifier as a literal. A value led by an angle span is now code when such an expression continuation follows it."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T13:26:40.481Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 16 on PR 1746: the type-assertion findings kept arriving one operator at a time (call, optional call, nullish, logical fallback, index), so the family is now closed structurally. After an assignment's equals sign, a value led by an angle span is a TypeScript type assertion or generic, or a shell redirection, never a placeholder, so the referenced reading treats it as code. After a colon (YAML, prose) an angle placeholder before a literal is still judged, which keeps the acceptance example. The inner-match closing-delimiter shortcut now also covers a quoted inner variable inside a command substitution."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T13:40:04.679Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 17 on PR 1746: a binary operator after a reference in unquoted source was read as the start of a credential literal. The code-after-reference rules are now one rule. In unquoted source a value is code when an operator or member access follows its leading references, when a call's parenthesis follows it, or, after an equals sign, when an angle span leads it. A letter, digit or quoted piece after a reference is still a literal concatenation. This subsumes the member-access check. One policy row now spells the CONSTANT_CASE concatenation with a quoted piece, because a hyphen after a reference reads as subtraction."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T13:50:19.877Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 18 on PR 1746: a key inside a command substitution that another reference follows kept its variable as literal text, because the closing parenthesis only ended the inner word before a quote. For a match the rescan found inside a rejected reference value, the closing parenthesis or brace of that reference now ends the word whatever follows. At the top level such a character is still literal data."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T14:02:41.036Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Greptile reviewed the intermediate heads of PR 1746 (seven P1s). Classified against main. Fixed, being regressions against main: a type assertion in an object property followed by expression code (an angle-led value is now code when expression code follows it, whatever the separator), and member access after nested references (leading references now read one nesting level, as the value grammar does). Fixed, being cheap and correct: the operator rule no longer treats the exclamation mark, at sign, hash, caret or tilde as binary operators, so a YAML literal led by them after a reference is judged; and a quoted array element counts as structured text, so a compact JSON array holding a bare variable stays clean. Declined, recorded as residuals on pm-jcz7qz because main behaves the same: a YAML literal after a reference that ends with an opening parenthesis (indistinguishable from a call), a quoted option name inside a command substitution visited by the structured pass, and a closing brace that is literal text inside a command substitution."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T14:17:32.822Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 20 on PR 1746. P1, fixed: the leading-reference matcher repeated an alternation once per character, so a reference of about 16 million characters overflowed the regex backtrack stack, the scan threw, and the guard failed open, letting an unrelated password in the same text through block mode. It is now an iterative scan (leadingReferencesEnd and referenceEnd) that reads each character once and follows nesting to any depth; a 16-million-character regression row and direct cases for unclosed brackets, reopened angle spans and a dollar without a name were added. P2, fixed: expression code after a type assertion may sit on the next line, so the expression and call look-aheads now skip any whitespace, not only blanks."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T14:30:42.012Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 21 on PR 1746, two fixes. (1) Type assertions in object properties followed by an operator or the as keyword were still read as literals, so the assertion family is now closed by shape: in unquoted source, an angle span that holds a TypeScript type (a primitive keyword, or a PascalCase name, optionally generic or an array type) leads code whatever follows it. Prose placeholders such as a lowercase single word keep their placeholder reading after a colon. (2) The reference-boundary scan counted a parenthesis inside a quoted argument as nesting; it now skips quoted spans and escapes, as the expansion walker does. The scanner was split into referenceEnd, angleSpanEnd and bracketedEnd to stay within the complexity limit."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T14:47:03.843Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "CodeRabbit (one Major, one Minor) and Greptile (three P1s) on the 55bb676a3 to 9774e3cdb heads. Major, fixed: a key inside a rejected reference ended its word at any parenthesis or brace, so a parenthesis inside a braced reference hid a literal suffix. Each inner match now records where the closer of its innermost enclosing reference stands, computed in one linear walk of the rejected value plus a bounded, quote-aware search for a reference the value grammar cut short, and its word ends only exactly there. Minor, already fixed by the iterative leading-reference scan; a row now proves it. Greptile, fixed: XOR after a reference is code again; a field separator alone after an angle placeholder ends flow-YAML data, so the expression rule keeps only optional chains, nullish and logical operators, calls and indexes; and ALL-CAPS angle names are placeholders by convention, so only primitive keywords, type parameters and PascalCase names count as TypeScript types."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T15:01:11.445Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 23 on PR 1746, two fixes. (1) The reference-span walk skipped only single-quoted spans inside a reference, so a double-quoted closer inside a command substitution was taken for its closer; it now skips both quote kinds and escapes. (2) Acronym-prefixed type names such as APIKey or IAuthToken were not recognized as TypeScript types; a capitalized angle name now counts as a type when it holds any lowercase letter or is a single letter, so only ALL-CAPS names stay placeholders. Performance: the span walk now runs only once the rescan finds a key inside a rejected value, which keeps the 16-million-character controls within their bounds under instrumentation."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T15:10:25.311Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 24 on PR 1746: skipping a double-quoted span wholesale hid a command substitution nested inside it, so a password variable inside that nested substitution was mapped to the outer closer and reported. The span walk now keeps quote state per open reference, as the expansion walker does: inside a reference a single-quoted span is inert, and a double-quoted span ignores the reference's closer but still opens nested references, each with its own quote state."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T15:30:15.213Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 25 on PR 1746, two fixes. (1) Qualified and union TypeScript types (a dotted name, a union with undefined) were not recognized. Assertion recognition now reads the angle piece's text: a primitive keyword, a qualified name, a union or intersection, an array type, a type parameter or a capitalized name holding a lowercase letter is a type; a lowercase word, a hyphenated placeholder and an ALL-CAPS name stay placeholders. (2) Performance: each command substitution the value grammar cut short searched its own 4,096-character window, so 256,000 unmatched openers took about 8 seconds against 76 ms on main. All cut references are now resolved by one continuation of the same span walk with one shared 4,096-character budget; the same input now takes about 0.2 seconds, and a pinned row keeps it in the linear-time controls."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T15:41:57.594Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 26 on PR 1746: a TypeScript non-null assertion after a reference, followed by member access or an operator, was judged as credential text. A postfix exclamation mark right after the leading references now passes over to the character after it, so it is code when an operator or member access follows; an exclamation mark that leads literal data after a reference (YAML) is still judged."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T15:56:46.833Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 27 on PR 1746 found a P1 fail-open, fixed, together with a pre-existing one it exposed. (1) The single-ampersand allowance had turned the credential literal run into an alternation repeated once per character, so a literal of 16 million characters after a reference overflowed the regex stack and evaluateMutationGuard failed open (secret_guard_scan_failed_open), letting an unrelated password through block mode. Runs are a plain character class again and the single ampersand is its own bounded piece. (2) Bisecting the remaining overflow showed that V8 compiles an at-least-eight count on a negated class that includes whitespace with a backtrack entry per character: the quoted value grammar overflowed on a 16-million-character quoted value, which the published 2026.11.17 package also does (measured against the npm tarball). All four negated-class at-least-eight counts are now spelled as eight then zero-or-more, which is equivalent and stack-safe; the linear-time controls include unquoted, ampersand and quoted 16-million-character values."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T16:09:55.781Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 28 on PR 1746: a quoted reference followed by a non-null assertion, XOR or subtraction opened a shell concatenation, so the code after it was judged as a credential. After a quoted piece that holds a reference, a concatenation now continues only with a letter, digit, underscore, at sign, hash, percent, tilde, or an exclamation mark that no operator, member access or call follows. Caret and hyphen no longer continue it."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T16:22:24.333Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 29 on PR 1746, two fixes. (1) The rescan read a key word that names a braced reference's parameter as an inner assignment, so a bash substring expression whose parameter is a password variable was reported; a key that starts at the parameter-name position of a braced reference (after the opening brace, or after a length or indirection sign) is now skipped. (2) A remainder operator after a quoted reference opened a concatenation; the percent sign no longer continues it."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T16:34:34.223Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Greptile on the latest heads of PR 1746 (three P1s, none a regression against main). Fixed: skipping a parameter-name key also skipped its value, so a password assignment inside the default word of that reference was missed; the rescan now resumes right after the skipped key. Declined, recorded on pm-jcz7qz: a YAML literal that starts with an exclamation mark and a dot right after a reference reads as a non-null assertion and member access, and a referenced credential-key value with more than 2,048 single ampersands is read up to the 4,096-piece bound."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T16:58:48.661Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "CodeRabbit full review of 539a18249 (two minors). Fixed: bitwise AND directly after a reference in assignment source is code; the ampersand joins the operator set for the character right after the leading references, while a single ampersand later in YAML data stays data. Declined: preserving an escaped dollar sign in a decoded string literal as a non-reference. The escape's meaning depends on the host language (in a JavaScript string or template literal it yields a plain dollar sign, and only shell double quotes make it literal), and preserving it made the repository scan flag our own template-literal fixtures whose escaped interpolation spells a shell reference. Decoding keeps reading an escaped dollar as a dollar sign, as JSON and JavaScript readers do, and a whole-value variable stays a placeholder under the documented policy; recorded as residual (15) on pm-jcz7qz."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T17:14:23.623Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 32 on PR 1746, two fixes. (1) Inside a command substitution, a shell control operator (pipe, ampersand, semicolon) right after an inner variable did not end its word, so a password variable piped to a filter was counted as literal text; for a match the rescan found inside a rejected reference value, those operators now end the word. (2) TypeScript literal types (a quoted string or a number, alone or in a union) were not recognized as type assertions; they now are."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T17:29:12.490Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 33 on PR 1746: inside a command substitution a redirection after an inner variable did not end its word, and a double ampersand that the password grammar reads as value characters was glued onto the inner value. For a match the rescan found inside a rejected reference value, the judged value is now cut at its first unquoted shell operator or redirection (pipe, ampersand, semicolon, angle brackets) outside nested references, and the word ends there."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T17:45:58.767Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 34 on PR 1746 found another stack-overflow fail-open: the literal grammar's member-path, hyphenated-word and path exclusions repeated groups without bound, so a referenced value with millions of member segments threw and the whole payload failed open. Two fixes. (1) Every repeated group in those exclusions is now bounded at 4,096, so a longer chain is simply not excluded. (2) Defense in depth in the SDK guard: a rule whose pattern exhausts the regex stack on one value now reports that value under the rule with normalization_incomplete for explicit review, in both the leaf pass and the member pass, and keeps scanning every other leaf; any other detector error still yields the fail-open advisory. Measured against the published 2026.11.17 package, five of six multi-million-character credential-key shapes (quoted member chains, hyphenated words, paths, unquoted member chains and paths) already threw there, so the per-leaf fail-closed behavior also closes that pre-existing fail-open. New spec secret-guard-oversized-values.spec.ts covers both paths with mocked overflows."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T18:00:38.271Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "CodeQL and Greptile on a647d3e5c, three fixes. (1) CodeQL alert 62 and a Greptile P1: the literal-type union pattern let a numeric member match two alternatives, so a crafted union backtracked exponentially (24 members took about 2.7 seconds). Every member alternative now starts with a different character class, so the union is read one way; 5,000 members take a few milliseconds. (2) An operator inside quoted text within a command substitution, and (3) an operator inside a braced reference's word, were taken for shell separators. Each reference span now records its kind and its own quoted ranges; an inner match is in shell context only when its innermost enclosing reference is a command substitution and the match lies outside that reference's quoted ranges (binary search), and only then do shell operators end or cut its word."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T18:28:42.150Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "CodeRabbit full review of 2f31e7fcd, two fixes. (1) A JSON scalar sibling (a number, true, false or null) after a quoted array element was not taken for structure, so a compact JSON array holding a bare variable followed by a scalar was flagged; a complete scalar that a delimiter ends now starts the next element, bare or after an opening brace or bracket, while a digit-led word still continues the shell word. (2) Test isolation: the member-pass error assertions could pass through the leaf pass; member-only sentinels now throw only in the member judge, and the assertions pin the member pass's own fail-closed findings (both key-named rules that read the name) and its error propagation."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T18:52:47.071Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Greptile on faaa70a88: the bare-scalar allowance also applied after a quoted option name in a shell command, so a quoted password variable followed by a comma, a number and a literal lost the literal. The structured context is now split: a bare scalar sibling is structure only after an actual array element (the value follows an opening bracket or a comma); after a quoted key alone only a quote, or an opening brace or bracket that structure follows, starts the next member."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T19:06:57.103Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 38 on PR 1746: a quoted shell option name still counted as a structured key, so a quoted password variable followed by a comma and a quoted literal lost the literal. A quoted key now counts as structured only as an object member: the key's opening quote follows an opening brace or a comma. A quoted shell option is not one, so a sibling-looking suffix continues the shell argument."
